arXiv:2606.30435v2 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: Let $Q=\{q_n\}_{n\geq1}$ be a sequence of integers with $q_n\geq2$ for every $n\geq1$. Every $x\in[0,1)$ admits a Cantor series expansion of the form $$ x
  =
  \frac{\varepsilon_1(x)}{q_1}
  +
  \frac{\varepsilon_2(x)}{q_1q_2}
  +\cdots+
  \frac{\varepsilon_n(x)}{q_1q_2\cdots q_n}
  +\cdots.$$In this paper, we study exact approximation of real numbers by the $n$-th partial sums of their Cantor series expansions. More precisely, for a non-increasing function $\psi:\mathbb{N}\to(0,\infty)$ satisfying $\psi(n)\to0$, let $E_c(\psi)$ be the set of points that are $\psi$-approximable by their $n$-th partial sums but are not $b\psi$-approximable for any $0<b<1$. We determine the Hausdorff dimension of $E_c(\psi)$.
